NNAAMMEE
       renice - alter priority of running processes

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       rreenniiccee [--nn] _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y [--gg|--pp|--uu] _i_d_e_n_t_i_f_i_e_r...

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       rreenniiccee alters the scheduling priority of one or more running processes.
       The first argument is the _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y value to be used. The other
       arguments are interpreted as process IDs (by default), process group
       IDs, user IDs, or user names. rreenniiccee'ing a process group causes all
       processes in the process group to have their scheduling priority
       altered. rreenniiccee'ing a user causes all processes owned by the user to
       have their scheduling priority altered.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --nn, ----pprriioorriittyy _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y
           Specify the scheduling _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y to be used for the process, process
           group, or user. Use of the option --nn or ----pprriioorriittyy is optional, but
           when used it must be the first argument.

       --gg, ----ppggrrpp
           Interpret the succeeding arguments as process group IDs.

       --pp, ----ppiidd
           Interpret the succeeding arguments as process IDs (the default).

       --uu, ----uusseerr
           Interpret the succeeding arguments as usernames or UIDs.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

FFIILLEESS
       _/_e_t_c_/_p_a_s_s_w_d
           to map user names to user IDs

NNOOTTEESS
       Users other than the superuser may only alter the priority of processes
       they own. Furthermore, an unprivileged user can only _i_n_c_r_e_a_s_e the "nice
       value" (i.e., choose a lower priority) and such changes are
       irreversible unless (since Linux 2.6.12) the user has a suitable "nice"
       resource limit (see uulliimmiitt(1p) and ggeettrrlliimmiitt(2)).

       The superuser may alter the priority of any process and set the
       priority to any value in the range -20 to 19. Useful priorities are: 19
       (the affected processes will run only when nothing else in the system
       wants to), 0 (the "base" scheduling priority), anything negative (to
       make things go very fast).

HHIISSTTOORRYY
       The rreenniiccee command appeared in 4.0BSD.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       The following command would change the priority of the processes with
       PIDs 987 and 32, plus all processes owned by the users daemon and root:

       rreenniiccee ++11 998877 --uu ddaaeemmoonn rroooott --pp 3322

